<Base title="Privacy · Yorkshire School Checker" description="What the Yorkshire School Checker does with your postcode, your list of schools and any message you send us. No account, anonymous visit counts, and analytics cookies you can turn off.">
  <section class="how-hero wrap">
    <div>
      <h1>Your privacy</h1>
      <p class="lede">There’s no account, and you can turn our analytics cookies off at any time. We only ask for your name and email if you send us a message, and our own counts are anonymous daily totals. Here’s exactly where your postcode goes and what stays on your phone.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="how-sec sec-pale wrap">
    <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
    <p class="intro">Your last search and your list of schools are saved in your browser, so they’re still there when you come back. They’re never sent to us. To remove them, clear this site’s data in your browser settings.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="how-sec wrap">
    <h2>Where your postcode goes</h2>
    <p class="intro">To answer your search, your browser asks three outside services for help. Each one sees your IP address, as any website does, and has its own privacy policy.</p>
    <div class="sources">
      <div><h3>Postcode lookup</h3><p>Your postcode goes to <a href="https://postcodes.io/about" target="_blank" rel="noopener">postcodes.io</a>, which turns it into a point on the map. Nothing else is sent.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Walking distances</h3><p>That map point goes to the <a href="https://routing.openstreetmap.de/about.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FOSSGIS routing service</a>, which works out walking routes to nearby schools using OpenStreetMap.</p></div>
      <div><h3>The map</h3><p>Map pictures come from <a href="https://www.esri.com/en-us/privacy/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Esri</a>. Your browser asks for the part of the map you’re looking at.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Your current location</h3><p>Only used if you tap “Use my current location” and your browser asks you first. It’s then used in place of a postcode, so it goes to the walking distance service but not the postcode lookup.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="how-sec sec-pale wrap" id="cookies">
    <h2>Analytics cookies, and how to turn them off</h2>
    <p class="intro">We use Google Analytics to count visits: which pages are viewed, roughly where visitors are (town or region, not your address), what device and browser they use, and how they found the site. We only use it to see what helps parents and to improve the site.</p>
    <p class="intro">It sets two cookies, <code>_ga</code> and <code>_ga_NR6T0LHP0J</code>, so it can tell a returning visitor from a new one. They last up to two years unless you clear them or turn them off. UK law allows cookies like these, used only for statistics to improve a website, without asking first, as long as we tell you and you can turn them off easily.</p>
    <p class="intro">Google handles the data on our behalf and only to give us reports. We’ve switched off Google’s advertising features and its use of the data for its own products, and Google keeps the detailed records for two months before deleting them. We never send your postcode, your list of schools or your name. You can read <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google’s privacy policy</a>.</p>
    <p class="intro"><b>To turn them off</b>, <button type="button" class="cc-inline" data-cookie-settings>open your cookie settings</button> and choose “Turn off”, or use Cookie settings at the bottom of any page. Google Analytics then sends nothing more from your browser and its cookies are deleted. Your choice is remembered in your browser.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="how-sec wrap" style="border-top:1px solid var(--line)">
    <h2>Hosting and who runs this</h2>
    <p class="intro">The site is delivered by Cloudflare, which handles visitors’ IP addresses to serve pages and block attacks, as every web host does.</p>
    <p class="intro">Separately from Google Analytics, and whatever you choose, we count page views, searches by area (never the postcode), how often each school’s details are opened, how often sponsored listings are seen and clicked, and which site a visit came from (just its name, such as “google”, never the page you were on). Only daily totals are kept, like “120 views of the plan page today”. These counts use no cookies, nothing is linked to you or your device, and your IP address isn’t stored.</p>
    <p class="intro">Some pages show listings from local businesses, marked “Sponsored”. They’re plain links: no ad networks, no cookies, and sponsors never learn who you are. Each link carries a tag naming this site, so the business can count visits from us.</p>
    <p class="intro">If you send us a message with the “Contact us” form, your name, email and message are emailed to us so we can reply. We don’t keep them on the site, and we don’t add you to a mailing list.</p>
    <p class="intro">If you send an advertising enquiry from our advertise page, your name, business and contact details are emailed to us so we can reply. We don’t add you to a mailing list.</p>
    <p class="intro">The Yorkshire School Checker is an independent tool made by <a href="https://www.mammothwebsites.co.uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mammoth Websites</a>. For questions about this page, get in touch through their website.</p>
    <p class="caveat">Last updated 12 November 2026.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
